// @test: run
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, project, mount, cross-file, name-resolution, T0907
// @timeout: 60000
// @project: module_leaf_collision
// @expected-stdout: 103
//
//   THIS SPEC LANDED SKIPPED AND THE SKIP CAME OFF, which is the record
//   of what happened between. When it was written it named ONE defect
//   and uncovered a second underneath: `@project:` needs a cog, and a
//   cog collected the module TWICE with two different function ids, so
//   the qualified key landed on the body-less first pass and the call
//   still returned `()`. The trace told both stories in three lines:
//
//       bind_mounted_function   key='verifier.verify'
//       decl-takeback-foreign   mine=33895 holder=Some(33349)
//       decl-yield              mine=33896 holder=Some(33895)
//
//   Line two is T0907 repaired — the stranger 33349 loses the key.
//   Line three was T1110: the same declaration registering a second
//   time under a NEW id and yielding to its own first pass. The
//   loose-file form of this exact source printed 103 on the same
//   binary, which is how the two were told apart. With T1110 fixed the
//   third line reads `mine=33895 holder=Some(33895)` — holder IS mine,
//   the yield is a no-op, and the collection is idempotent.
// @description: A module keeps its own function when its name is also the
//               last segment of a stdlib module's path.
//
//   `core/` ships three modules whose path ends in `.verifier`
//   (`core.security.x509.verifier`, `core.security.zk.halo2.verifier`,
//   `core.security.zk.stark.verifier`), and each declares `verify`. A user
//   module named `verifier` declaring its own `verify` therefore contests a
//   name it has every right to. Measured 2026-09-03 (T0907), one binary:
//
//       module verifier;        print(verify(3))  ->  ()
//       module probe.verifier;  print(verify(3))  ->  103
//
//   Only the module's SPELLING differs. The second is the cog-qualified
//   form, which collides with nothing; the first is the plain one, and it
//   lost. With a `Result` return the damage is quieter still — the `match`
//   took the `Err` arm of a function that returns `Ok` unconditionally, so
//   the program printed a wrong number rather than nothing, and `check`,
//   `build` and `run` all reported zero diagnostics.
//
//   ROOT. A module's own declaration owns `<its module>.<its name>`. The
//   ownership test asked two questions of whoever already held the key —
//   is it a mount? is it an extern stub? — and read every OTHER holder as
//   the one legitimate case: a second local declaration of the same module,
//   which keeps source-order precedence. A third case existed, a foreign
//   module holding the key, and it was read as the first. The declaration
//   yielded its own name, and the explicit `mount` then found the stranger
//   on the ladder's first probe and bound the bare alias to it.
//
//   The holder was shown to be foreign rather than a second pass over the
//   same declaration by perturbation, not by inspection: padding the user's
//   module with three extra declarations walked its own id 33895 -> 33898
//   while the holder stayed 33349.
//
//   WHY 103 AND NOT A BOOLEAN. The expectation is deliberately a value the
//   stdlib's `verify` cannot produce. Any of them returns a verdict, not
//   `n + 100`, so this spec cannot pass by accidentally calling the right
//   name on the wrong function.
//
//   CONTROL. `two_file_project_runs.vr` in this directory is the same shape
//   with a non-colliding module name. If both fail, multi-file projects are
//   broken in general and this spec says nothing about collisions; if only
//   this one fails, the collision is the cause.

fn main() {
    // The program lives in the sibling directory named by `@project:`.
    // This body is never compiled — vtest hands the compiler that
    // directory, not this file.
}
